body: "## What was measured\n\n`scripts/release/tracker-measurement-gate.mjs` reports every enforced bound with its observed value. On 2026-08-10 the `dependency-kind-blocks` declaration (owner pm-jkbqt8) reads ceiling 243 against an observed 243: **zero headroom**. Fourteen of the twenty-nine enforced bounds sit at zero headroom, but this one is different in kind, because another open item's acceptance criteria require the population to grow.\n\n## The conflict\n\n`blocks` and `blocked_by` are the only registered relationship kinds whose contract sets `ordering: true`. Delivery sequence is therefore inexpressible in any other kind.\n\npm-1w6scp requires, verbatim, that \"the original milestones carry their delivery order as typed edges so a traversal can reconstruct the sequence without parsing titles\". pm-gxs55a records that 1,763 of 1,991 sequenced nodes have no predecessor at all. pm-ye46ns exists to recover sequence edges for terminal work from prerequisite statements already recorded in the tracker.\n\nEvery one of those three requires adding `blocks` rows. The ceiling refuses the first one.\n\n## Why the ceiling exists, and why that is not wrong\n\nThe ceiling was filed to stop the failure mode where a relation that merely *feels* directional gets minted as `blocks`, inflating the ordering graph with edges that carry no scheduling meaning. That is a real and recurring failure mode and the ceiling is the correct instrument for it.\n\nThe defect is not the ceiling's existence. It is that the ceiling is **unqualified**: it counts every `blocks` row identically, so an evidence-cited ordering edge recovered from a recorded prerequisite statement is indistinguishable from a fabricated one. A single global count cannot separate the population the ceiling wants to suppress from the population another accepted item requires.\n\n## Consequence\n\nThe ordering half of the historical-spine reconstruction is unlandable as specified. It is not blocked by missing evidence or missing tooling; it is blocked by an enforcement mechanism that cannot express the distinction the work depends on. Neither item names the other, so the deadlock is invisible from either side: pm-1w6scp reads as ready, and the declaration reads as satisfied.\n\n## Dispositions available\n\n1. **Partition the selector.** Let a declaration scope to edges carrying a provenance marker (`source_kind`), so fabricated `blocks` stays ceilinged while evidence-cited ordering is counted separately and floored. This is the only option that keeps both properties enforced.\n2. **Re-declare with a recorded reason.** Raise the ceiling once, in the same change that lands the ordering tranche, with a note naming the evidence rule that justifies the new value. Cheap, but it spends the ceiling's whole protective value in one step.\n3. **Restate pm-1w6scp.** Drop the ordering criterion and accept that delivery sequence stays unreadable. This should be chosen only if the sequence is judged not worth recording.\n\nOption 1 is the one consistent with the polarity work in pm-g4k74y: a bound should be able to say which population it speaks for."
